00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:40.360
larger companies, it might actually lead

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.400
to a much bigger problem as it follows

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.960
something called a negative momentum

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.760
approach, which sounds complicated, but

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.320
it's really not. All it means is that

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:51.800
when a company in the S&P 500 starts to

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:54.000
take off, think Tesla during its big

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.240
run, the fund automatically sells some

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.680
of it as it rises and uses that money to

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.560
buy companies that haven't gone up. So,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.000
you're effectively selling the winners

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.360
and buying the losers purely to keep

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.600
everything balanced. On the flip side, a

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:10.720
normal S&P 500 index fund that's market

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:12.760
cap weighted doesn't need to trade

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.880
nearly as often. When a company gains

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:17.000
momentum, it's simply allowed to take up

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.120
a bigger share of the index. Instead of

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.840
trimming the winners, the fund gradually

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.160
reduces exposure to smaller companies to

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.120
make room for the larger ones. But why

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.920
does this matter? Well, the more a fund

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:29.480
trades, the higher the costs are.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.080
Therefore, it's something that's often

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.360
overlooked that can eat [music] into

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:36.160
